# No pool positioning (HARD — copy law)

**Owner letter 2026-08-07.** Customers bought Barreletics for pool use and **slipped on wet tile**. Pool language attracts the wrong buyer and creates liability.

## Banned — never write these

pool · poolside · pool deck · "around the pool" · "in the pool" · water park · waterpark · tidal pool · tidepool · spa visits · aqua barre · water aerobics

Also banned as SEO tags: `grippy shoes for pools`, `pool shoes`, `pool deck shoes`.

## Say instead

**resortwear · paddleboarding · beach · outdoor yoga**

Supporting terms that remain approved: boating · boat deck · hot sand · travel · surf.

## Don't invent scenery

**Beach covers it.** Do not write rocky coves · rocky coastline · rocky shorelines · tidepools · shell-covered beaches · pebbly lake beds · wet shoreline. Nobody talks like that.

## Scope (all of it)

FAQ blocks · GEO blocks · product descriptions · SEO/meta tags · collection copy · blog · email · ManyChat KB · ads · mocks in `docs/` · every operating-system doc in `planning/`, `specs/`, `manychat-kb/`.

Applies to `shopify-build/**` runtime JSON (notably `templates/collection.outdoor.json` and any `collection-faq` / `geo-section` blocks).

## Safety caveat stays — just reworded

Keep the wet-surface caution, but name the surface, not the venue:

```
✅ "Certain wet tile and stone areas are inherently slippery for any footwear."
❌ "Certain spa/pool/tile areas..."
```

## Exceptions

- `docs/08-LIVE-SITE-COPY-AUDIT.md` is an **evidence snapshot** of live copy — quoted pool text stays as a record, under the retired-language banner. Never reuse it as source copy.
- `backups/**` and `archive/**` are frozen snapshots — do not edit.

## Still live on Shopify (Admin fix, not repo)

**Verified against the live site 2026-08-12** — the earlier version of this list named the blog posts imprecisely:

- **Water Shoes description** (`/products/aquatic-performance-skins`): "Ideal for paddle-boarding, boating and **poolside** activities." Shopify echoes this into JSON-LD on **every page**, so one field is the source of sitewide "poolside" hits. Fix this first.
- **`/blogs/news/barre-anywhere`** — an aqua barre article that pitches the product for pool use. The actual liability. There is **no** post titled "aqua barre."
- **`/blogs/news/eating-healthy-…-performance-in-the-water`** — aquatic-athlete nutrition. Off-brand, low risk, doesn't recommend the shoes on wet surfaces.
- Product FAQ "Great for" list · FAQ caution line — **not re-verified**; check before acting.

